@@ -11,7 +11,20 @@ The one-byte test vector exercises the encoder boundary; it is deliberately not
 a claim that a one-byte TPDU is a valid SMS. The 164-byte limit follows the
 upstream oFono `sms_encode()` buffer contract (164 TPDU + 12 SMSC bytes).
 Retry, message-reference and acknowledgment behavior is unchanged.
+`make -C tests test-paired BINDER_CORE=/absolute/path/to/patched/core` additionally
+feeds the production core splitter's output into the production extension
+encoder and both captured writers. It still performs no Binder operations.
 
-The standalone TPDU/technology/privacy patch rejects non-null SMSC at the
-extension entry point until the separately reviewed caller encoding patch is
-applied. This is an experimental branch, not a deployable/no-send policy.
+The standalone TPDU/technology/privacy patch rejects non-null SMSC. The
+separately reviewable paired patch opts in to the new
+`BINDER_EXT_SMS_INTERFACE_FLAG_SMSC_HEX` contract: the patched Binder core encodes
+explicit-length SMSC bytes (including the length octet) as hex before handing
+it to the string API. The core keeps legacy representation for unflagged
+extensions. NULL/empty SMSC becomes the HAL empty string. Binary, odd/non-hex,
+oversized and inconsistent-length strings are rejected, not guessed.
+
+Build this opt-in extension against the paired core headers; require that core
+at runtime for explicit SMSC support. Old cores ignore the new flag and pass
+binary SMSC, which this extension rejects. The core can fall back on submission
+failure: rejection is NOT a no-send policy or a guarantee against fallback.
+This remains an experimental branch, not a deployable telephony package.
